{"input": "", "answers": ["Both of TV's \"Fat Ladies\" have sung. Clarissa Dickson Wright, one half of the BBC's \"Two Fat Ladies\" cooking duo, died in Edinburgh Saturday at age 66, the BBC reports. Wright was a former lawyer who filmed four of the \"Fat Ladies\" series, going on food-related road trips across the UK in a motorbike and sidecar with Jennifer Paterson, before Paterson died in 1999 from cancer. The New York Times describes Wright as a \"rebel,\" both hosts as \"irreverent and eccentric,\" and the recipes as \"sometimes confounding.\" Wright's eclectic working life also included stints as a cook, an author, and a cookbook shop manager; she also ran a catering business, was a guild butcher, and once worked on a yacht in the Caribbean. In fact, she recently said, \"I've had a fantastic life and I've done everything I could have wanted to do and more.\" It wasn't until her 40s, after she'd recovered from alcoholism, that she got into cooking seriously. As for the perhaps-controversial title of the show that brought her fame? \"If you're fat you're fat,\" she once said. \"I hate this modern-day political correctness, that you don't call things by their proper name.\" Her agent remembers Wright similarly in a statement: \"Loved dearly by her friends and many fans all over the world, Clarissa was utterly non-PC and fought for what she believed in, always, with no thought to her own personal cost.\" There's no word on Wright's cause of death, but the Guardian reports that she had been undergoing treatment at a hospital since the beginning of the year."], "length": 2870, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Clarissa Dickson Wright, one half of the culinary duo Two Fat Ladies, has died in hospital in Edinburgh aged 66. She was a brilliant cook, an erudite food historian and a champion of hunting and shooting once convicted of attending an illegal hare coursing event. She had no time for rudeness and little for those whose philosophies she could not understand, including Labour politicians and'manky' vegetarians. But above all, she became a great British eccentric who made a unlikely television star as one half of the BBC's Two Fat Ladies partnership. The worlds of food and television paid tribute to Dickson Wright on Monday following her death in hospital in Edinburgh, where she had been undergoing treatment since the start of the year. Producer Patricia Llewellyn, who teamed her up with Jennifer Paterson for the show (in which they toured the UK in a motorcycle-sidecar), said: 'Clarissa was a marvellous cook and knew food and food history inside out. She possessed a formidable intelligence, a strong hold on opinions, a powerful combination that made her a commanding presence on television.' Dickson Wright's agents, Heather Holden-Brown and Elly James, said her fun, laughter and intelligence would be missed. The Observer's restaurant critic Jay Rayner tweeted: 'Goodbye Clarissa. A truly fabulous woman. Worked with her many times. It was always fun, at least for me. She had a towering intellect.' Television chef Jamie Oliver added: 'She was always entertaining to watch and was of course a passionate foodie.' Born Clarissa Theresa Philomena Aileen Mary Josephine Agnes Elsie Trilby Louise Esmerelda Dickson Wright in 1947, she grew up in north London. Her father, Dr Arthur, was a brilliant surgeon who treated members of the royal family - and was also an abusive and alcoholic. She became a barrister but descended into alcoholism and then sobered up, getting serious about cooking in her 40s. She was writing a cooking column and running a store called Books for Cooks in London when she was recruited to collaborate with another culinary rebel, Jennifer Paterson, on a cooking show unlike any other. Two Fat Ladies made its debut on BBC Two in 1996 and was picked up in the United States the next year by the Food Network. Each episode opened with the pair heading to a new location to cook, Paterson steering a motorcycle while Dicksonson rode in a sidecar, sometimes beneath the carcass of an animal"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A hiker's fatal fall in March has been blamed on using a smartphone app map instead of an actual map. The BBC reports Jane Wilson and her husband Gary were hiking on Tryfan in Wales when it started to get dark and they decided to skip the summit. While attempting to descend the mountain, Gary was looking at an Ordnance Survey app while Jane went a short way ahead to make sure the path was safe, according to the Telegraph. The Manchester Evening News reports Gary heard his wife fall, got to safety, and called for help. Rescuers found the 53-year-old librarian's body at the bottom of a 30-foot cliff with a fractured skull. At a hearing Friday, Jane's was ruled an accidental death due, in part, to using an app instead of a map. \"Apart from potential difficulties caused by poor detail on an electronic map, batteries on mobile phones have a nasty habit of running out just when you need them most,\" the Telegraph quotes a spokesperson for Mountain Rescue England and Wales as saying. A detective says the app map \"would have been small and not as detailed\" and took the couple on a route that was not the safest. A director with Ordnance Survey says hikers should always use the app in conjunction with an actual map \"due to the nature of mobile devices when navigating the real world.\" Authorities also say Jane wasn't wearing appropriate footwear for the hike. (Go inside the futile search for \"Inchworm\" on the trail.)"], "length": 1566, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A woman fell to her death while using a smartphone app to find a safe way down from a peak in Snowdonia, an inquest has heard. Jane Wilson, 53, and her husband Gary, both experienced walkers, were on the mountain's west face when she fell 30ft down a vertical cliff. The coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death. The couple had been walking and scrambling for six years. Mrs Wilson, a librarian at Manchester University, fractured her skull and sustained other severe injuries in the fall. Her husband raised the alarm after climbing to a safer ledge. Members of the Ogwen Valley Mountain Rescue Team found her body. The inquest heard that the couple had been using a smartphone app to navigate the mountain. The coroner said there would have been less detail on the app than on a proper map. The couple had been walking and scrambling for six years. Mrs Wilson was a fit and active woman. The coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Mitch Daniels has some advice for those who are writing Rick Perry's political eulogy: Hold your horses. \"It's way too early to know, or to issue, to pronounce last rites over one performance,\" Daniels said today. \"There's still many of these (debates), too many maybe. ... I'd cut him some slack and give him a little time.\" Politico notes that Daniels also had kind words for Democrat Sen. Mark Warner's work on the Gang of Six, saying, \"It's very encouraging, by the way, to hear a guy like Sen. Warner speak to it as clearly as he just did. I wish he were president.\" More fun on your Sunday dial, as per Politico: Michael Bloomberg on President Obama's 2012 chances: \"He would be a credible, formidable candidate. You're going to have a real horse race no matter who the Republican nominee is.\" Reince Priebus to Debbie Wasserman Schultz on 2012: \"It sounds like the new slogan is no longer 'hope and change.' It's, 'Hey, it could've been worse.' Great bumper sticker Debbie, I hope it works for you.\" David Plouffe on a White House shakeup: \"I don't expect that.\" Obama is \"confident in his team, in the direction we've laid out here.\" Warner on Congress' standoff over a short-term spending bill: \"It is embarrassing. The Senate is saying, 'Why should we build schools in Iraq on the credit card but expect that rebuilding schools in Joplin, Mo.,'\" will be paid for. Warner blames \"a group, more centered in the House, in terms of some of these Tea Party Republicans, who say on every issue, 'We're going to make this a make-or-break.'\" Lindsey Graham on Pakistan: \"We need to put Pakistan on notice. We're going to have to put all options on the table. including defending our troops. It's now a time of choosing, so I hope they choose wisely.\""], "length": 1454, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "New York Mayor Michael Bloomberg said Sunday that President Barack Obama is a \"credible, formidable candidate\" for reelection and praised Rick Perry and Mitt Romney for the very things that have hindered them in the Republican presidential campaign. Indiana Gov. Mitch Daniels said Sunday that fellow Republicans should forgive Texas Gov. Rick Perry's uneven debate performances as the GOP presidential campaign goes forward. Republican National Committee Chairman Reince Priebus mocked President Obama's 2008 election slogan, saying it won't pass the smell test with voters in 2012. Sen. Mark Warner (D-Va.) said Sunday that the specter of a government shutdown because Congress can't agree on another short-term spending bill is \"embarrassing\" and blamed tea-party affiliated Republicans for the stalemate. Sen. Lindsey Graham (R-S.C.) said Sunday that the United States needs to leave \"all options on the table\" in dealing with Pakistan after U.S. military officials accused the country's intelligence officials of assisting terrorist operations. President Barack Obama's senior adviser, David Plouffe, said Sunday that he doesn't expect significant changes in top White House staff or the Cabinet ahead of the 2012 reelection campaign."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"That new soda tax in Seattle is working out about as well as Chicago's,\" proclaims a headline at Hot Air. It's not a compliment. The tax, which went into effect Jan. 1, slaps an additional 1.75 cents on each fluid ounce of sugar-sweetened drinks, a group that includes soda, sports drinks, and kombucha. That's nearly double the one-cent levy the Chicago-encompassing Cook County tried, a tax it repealed after about two months. The Chicago Tribune reported at the time that Costco's nine affected locations saw their sales of beverages impacted by the tax fall 34%, while sales jumped 38% at locations just beyond the county line. And Costco is again at the fore of what's happening: Because bulk purchases contain significantly more ounces than an individual one, the impact is starker, as KIRO discovered when it spotted an updated Costco sign for Gatorade in Seattle. The sign details Costco's price for the 35-pack of 16-ounce bottles—$15.99—and separately lists the city's $10.34 tax, for a new total of $26.33. But that's not all the sign says: It also directs consumers to nearby locations that are outside city limits and exempt from the tax. The tax is meant to fight obesity and raise funds for worthy expenditures, but the blog for Citizens Against Government Waste sees the ends quite differently: \"Let’s be clear. Soda taxes don’t make people healthier. They don't raise revenue—they drive it outside city limits. They don’t help ease inequality—they make the poor poorer.\" But some in government seemingly remain hopeful: KXLY reports a bill was reintroduced Monday that would push the tax statewide. (Speaking of Seattle and beverages, the world's largest Starbucks is no longer located there.)"], "length": 3161, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "Seattle's Sweetened Beverage Tax: A Burden on Low-Income Families and Small Businesses\n\nSeattle's sweetened beverage tax, which went into effect on January 1, 2018, has been criticized for its negative impact on low-income families and small businesses. The tax, which adds 1.75 cents per ounce to the price of sweetened beverages, has led to increased prices at Costco and other retailers. The tax does not apply to sugary drinks that list milk as their primary ingredient, such as flavored coffee beverages. Starbucks, which sells many sugar-heavy beverages, rejected the tax. The tax has also been criticized for not addressing the root causes of obesity and diabetes, such as lack of access to healthy food options and physical inactivity. The tax has been opposed by diverse groups, including labor unions, businesses, and low-income families. The tax has been described as a regressive tax that disproportionately affects low-income families. The tax has also been criticized for not raising revenue for the city, as people are buying drinks in the suburbs to avoid the tax. The tax has been seen as a failure in Cook County, Illinois, where the tax was repealed after a public outcry. The tax has also been criticized for not addressing the root causes of obesity and diabetes, such as lack of access to healthy food options and physical inactivity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A birthday party in California turned tragic after the guest of honor was run over and killed by his friend. Police say Jonathan Carlyle Merkley was celebrating his 34th birthday early Sunday at a hotel near San Diego when a woman decided to leave the party. Merkley wanted her to stay and reportedly walked toward her BMW and laid down in front of what the Orange County Register describes as a moving car. The woman didn’t stop, ran him over, and kept going. Police cite witnesses as saying both had been drinking, Fox 5 reports. Merkley, who suffered major chest trauma, died about 45 minutes later at a hospital. Police tracked the woman down and a car that \"may have been involved\" was impounded, cops tell the San Diego Union-Tribune. The woman was not arrested but police said on Tuesday that the investigation is continuing. \"There's a lot we still need to find out,\" says San Diego Police Sgt. Tim Underwood."], "length": 885, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A 34-year-old man was killed when he lay down in front of a car driven by his female friend as she drove away from his birthday party just after midnight Sunday, police said. Jonathan Carlyle Merkley, of Garden Grove, was celebrating his 34th birthday night at the Staybridge Suites hotel, in the 6600 block of Mira Mesa Boulevard, at about 12:30 a.m. According to San Diego Police Sgt. Tom Sullivan, the friends had been drinking alcohol at the hotel before the unidentified woman tried to get behind the wheel. Both had been drinking alcohol with friends before the incident. The man was trying to prevent the woman from leaving the hotel walked into her path and laid down in front of the moving car, according to San Diego Police Sgt. Sullivan. The woman did not stop, ran over him and kept going. The victim suffered major trauma to the chest and died later at a hospital, according to Sullivan. Police know the identity of the BMW driver, but she had not been arrested as of late Sunday mornin